>
> The best way to keep both yourself and your pet alive is to let him
> deal with most monsters for you.  The difficulty of generated monsters
> is based on your experience level (and the dungeon level you're on),
> not your pet's.  By letting him kill monsters, his level goes up while
> your level (and thus monster difficulty) stays low.
>
> Also remember that your pet probably has more HP than you.  Stay
> behind him and follow his footsteps; let him face the dangers first.
> No matter how good your pet is, it's no use having him stay alive if
> you yourself are dead.
>
> Of course, that's not to say you shouldn't gain any levels yourself --
> just don't be so eager to kill things yourself that you don't leave
> enough for your pet.  Of course, with such a tough pet so early,
> that's unlikely to be much of a problem.  In particular, now that you
> have a tough pet and are thus mostly safe from monsters, you should
> probably try to raise your maxHP high enough (that means over 22, I
> believe) that accidentally stepping into a land mine or a rolling
> boulder trap won't kill you immediately.  Gaining levels will make
> buying protection a bit more expensive, but with a pet mind flayer you
> shouldn't be having much trouble there either.
>
> A particular issue with mind flayers is that they can easily get
> themselves killed while attacking a golem or some other headless or
> brainless creature that's immune to their natural attacks.  You'll
> have to watch out for such fights and intervene before your pet gets
> the shit kicked out of himself.  Fortunately, mind flayers are at
> least smart enough not to attack cockatrices with their tentacles.
>
> > Another thing I'm wondering is, if I want to price-shop or buy
> > protection, I better have him on another level or he'll kill the guy
> > I'm trying to get stuff from--but how long can I do that before I
> > risk him going wild and killing me when I go back to fetch him?
>
> A well fed pet is a happy one, and won't turn on you even if you leave
> him alone for a while.  Just make sure you let him eat enough while
> he's with you.
>
> With mind flayers, this isn't likely to be a problem; not only are
> they omnivorous, but their brain-sucking attack feeds them as well.
> In fact, a more pressing concern may be to make sure you don't starve
> yourself while your pet eats all the tasty corpses before you can get
> to them.
>
